<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulln</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tulln an der Donau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tulln is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, on the site of the Roman fort of Comagena. It was a residence of the Babenberg margraves before they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klosterneuburg">Klosterneuburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> it is the place where Kriemhild meets Etzel. The painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egon-schiele">Egon Schiele</a> was born there in 1890.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tulln.jpg" alt="Tulln" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GuentherZ · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GuentherZ_2010-04-24_0034_Tulln_Hauptplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tulln lies in the centre of the Tulln Basin, which stretches to the Vienna Woods in the south, about 40 kilometres northwest of Vienna. Most of its built-up area is on the southern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Because of its many parks and gardens the town is often called Blumenstadt, the City of Flowers. The municipality consists of several cadastral communities, among them Langenlebarn, Staasdorf and Mollersdorf, and the town proper has about twelve thousand inhabitants. It is the administrative seat of Tulln District.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Tulln is presumably of Celtic origin, and the place was settled before Roman times. It gained importance as the Roman fort of Comagena, also called Comagenis. In the last years of Roman rule Saint Severinus of Noricum is said to have visited the town and saved it from the barbarians. A Roman tower of the 4th century still stands. The town is first mentioned as Tullina in a deed of 859, when the area belonged to the Bavarian marcha orientalis.</p><p>Tulln became important as a residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a>, who ruled as Austrian margraves from 976, and lost its significance after they moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. The first public clock tower is recorded here in 1372. On 6 September 1683 the Polish cavalry of King John III Sobieski joined Imperial forces and troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> at Tulln before the Battle of Vienna against the invading Ottoman army. In 1986 the town applied to become capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, but Sankt Pölten was chosen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tulln has a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-producing plant, and trade fairs, including camping equipment and boat shows, take place there. The Federal School of Aviation and several state institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, such as the fire brigade and the disaster relief service, have their seat in the town. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> it was at Tulln that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a> saw Kriemhild and proposed to her, and a Nibelungen Fountain was dedicated to the episode in 2005. The painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egon-schiele">Egon Schiele</a> was born in Tulln, which has a museum dedicated to him. The Minoritenkloster, a convent, is among the sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tulln an der Donau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their seat moved from Melk to <b>Tulln</b>, Klosterneuburg and finally Vienna around 1145.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
